within Pharmacolibrary.Drugs.C_CardiovascularSystem.C03X_OtherDiuretics.C03XA01_Tolvaptan;
model Tolvaptan_Van2006_PD_ufr
  "linear exposure-response sweep — Van_2006 :: UFR (urine flow rate)"
  extends Pharmacolibrary.Pharmacodynamic.LinearSweep(
    E0 = 1.46111111111e-08,      // [m3/s; mL/hr × 2.77778e-10]
    slope = 0.000261388888889,  // [meter ** 6 / kilogram / second; mL/hr per ng/mL × 0.000277778]
    exposure_per_s = 1e-06);   // 1 ng/mL per second
  annotation(experiment(StartTime = 0, StopTime = 1000, Interval = 2),
    Documentation(info = "<html><body><h4>Tolvaptan_Van2006_PD_ufr</h4><p>The steady-state exposure-response curve of Van Wart SA et al., — urine flow rate [mL/hr] against tolvaptan ng/mL — obtained by sweeping the driver instead of simulating a dose. Model family: linear.</p><h4>The parameters, in the paper's terms</h4><pre>  E0    =       52.6 mL/hr    drug-free response (baseline) (E0, Q324)
  slope =      0.941 mL/hr per ng/mL change in response per unit of exposure (Slope, Q335)</pre><p>The algebra is <a href=\"modelica://Pharmacolibrary.Pharmacodynamic.LinearSweep\">LinearSweep</a>; this model only supplies the numbers. The x axis is one ng/mL of tolvaptan per second of simulated time (exposure is in kg/m3): there is no state, no dose and no kinetics — plot <code>response</code> against <code>exposure</code> (or <code>time</code>).</p><h4>What it reproduces</h4><pre>  exposure = 0         response 1.461e-08   (drug-free)</pre></body></html>", revisions = "<html><body><ul><li>11/2026 generated by pharmacolibrary-dev export.pharmacolibrary_models from Van_2006: parameters extracted from the paper by the pharmacolibrary-dev pipeline, model built by its engineer, reviewed: simulated</li></ul></body></html>"));
end Tolvaptan_Van2006_PD_ufr;
